  "rationale": "structural: The structural obligations in `requirements.layout.project` are about where files live and how the project's own book is configured. Each has a root that fixes it.\n\n- **Rust source of the `habeas` crate in `src/`.** An alternative layout would keep the same behavior with the sources elsewhere, so this is structural. `Cargo.toml#table[lib]/pair[path]` (`src/lib.rs`) and `Cargo.toml#table[bin]/pair[path]` (`src/main.rs`) pin the crate's targets under `src/`. The package name is `habeas` (`Cargo.toml#table[package]/pair[name]`).\n- **mdBook Markdown in `md/`.** `book.toml#table[book]/pair[src]` (`src = \"md\"`) points the book at `md/`.\n- **`trust-but-verify` crate source in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/`.** `crates/trust-but-verify/Cargo.toml#table[package]/pair[name]` names the crate. The crate declares no custom target paths, so Cargo's default `src/` applies under `crates/trust-but-verify/`. The workspace member entry, `Cargo.toml#table[workspace]/pair[members]`, belongs to the child requirement `requirements.layout.project.workspace` and is not needed here.\n- **The book uses the Habeas preprocessor as specified by `requirements.cli.mdbook`.** `book.toml#table[preprocessor.habeas]/pair[command]` (`cargo habeas mdbook`) configures the `habeas` preprocessor. It is also marked for `requirements.cli.mdbook`. That requirement is only cited by this one, so its other obligations, including the Cargo alias, are judged there.\n\nThe five supplied roots cover these obligations. I found no further structural obligation that needs a root.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement has four obligations. Tests cover each one.\n\n1. **mdBook Markdown in `md/`.** `tests/repository.rs#fn[project_book_uses_the_repository_local_preprocessor_alias]` asserts that `book.toml` has `book.src == \"md\"`. It also asserts that `md/SUMMARY.md` exists.\n\n2. **`habeas` crate's Rust source in `src/`.** `tests/repository.rs#fn[crate_targets_are_declared_in_src]` asserts that the manifest declares the lib at `src/lib.rs`. It also asserts that there is exactly one bin, named `habeas`, at `src/main.rs`. The book test also checks that `src/lib.rs` and `src/main.rs` exist.\n\n3. **`trust-but-verify` crate's Rust source in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/`.** `tests/repository.rs#fn[workspace_contains_frontend_and_sdk_but_not_the_vendor_fork]` asserts that `crates/trust-but-verify/src/lib.rs` exists. It checks that the workspace members are exactly `habeas` and `trust-but-verify`. It also checks that the dependency path is `crates/trust-but-verify`.\n\n4. **The book uses the Habeas preprocessor as `requirements.cli.mdbook` specifies.** The book test asserts that the `habeas` preprocessor command is `cargo habeas mdbook` and that its renderers are `[\"habeas\"]`. It also asserts that the `.cargo/config.toml` alias `habeas` is `run --release -p habeas --`. This matches the cited requirement's project-specific clause. The cited requirement's other obligations are context only and are not reassessed here.\n\nA plausible violation would fail one of these assertions. Examples are moving the source directory, changing the lib or bin path, dropping the `trust-but-verify` source file, or changing the preprocessor command or alias. The text does not name other behavior, such as a check that no other stray Rust files exist. Such checks are not gaps. All three tests carry `{test}` markers for the subject.",
